# dconf -- the GSettings backend, and the reason settings survive a logout.
#
# NOT OPTIONAL IN PRACTICE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF PACKAGING IT. GSettings has
# a backend hierarchy: with dconf present it writes to the dconf database; with
# no dconf at all it falls back to the MEMORY BACKEND, which accepts every write
# and discards them when the process exits. Nothing fails, nothing warns, and
# every setting a program stores is silently lost.
#
# gnome-software keeps its own state in GSettings, so on a system without dconf
# it would forget everything between runs while appearing to work. flatpak also
# has a -Ddconf option, for migrating an application's settings into its
# sandbox.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/dconf/0.40/dconf-0.40.0.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=cf7f22a4c9200421d8d3325c5c1b8b93a36843650c9f95d6451e20f0bcb24533
SRC_DIR=dconf-0.40.0

# TWO OF THESE ARE HARD FAILURES RATHER THAN SILENT DEGRADATIONS, which makes
# this recipe the opposite of most in this chain -- the build stops rather than
# quietly shipping less.
#
# -Dbash_completion=false. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS TRUE, and meson.build then does
#     bash_completion_dep = dependency('bash-completion')
# with NO required:false. bash-completion is not packaged, so left at its default
# THIS FAILS CONFIGURE. Not payload avoidance -- a build requirement that cannot
# be met. (bash itself is packaged; the completion framework is a separate
# project and is not.)
#
# -Dvapi=false. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS TRUE and it needs vala, which is not
# packaged. Same shape: a default that cannot be satisfied here.
#
# -Dman=false. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS TRUE and it needs xsltproc and the docbook
# stylesheets, neither packaged.
#
# -Dgtk_doc is already false upstream and is not passed. Recorded rather than
# flagged, for the same reason as xdg-dbus-proxy's installed_tests: a recipe
# that says nothing cannot distinguish a checked default from an unexamined one.
#
# NOT SET: -Dsystemduserunitdir. meson.build takes dependency('systemd',
# required: false) and skips the unit when it is absent, which is correct here --
# Duct has elogind and no systemd, and there is no systemd user session for a
# unit to be installed into. dconf-service is D-Bus activated, which is how it
# starts without one.
MESON_ARGS="-Dbash_completion=false -Dvapi=false -Dman=false"
